<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Niue</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Niue is a raised coral island in the South Pacific, east of Tonga, one of the largest coral islands in the world. It became a British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1900, was annexed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> with the Cook Islands in 1901 and given its own administration in 1904. Since 1974 it has been self-governing in free association with New Zealand.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/niue.jpg" alt="Niue" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Niue.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Niue lies in a triangle drawn between Tonga, Samoa and the Cook Islands, about 2,400 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and 604 kilometres north-east of Tonga. It has a land area of about 261.46 square kilometres and is nicknamed The Rock, from its traditional name, the Rock of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>. The terrain has two levels: a limestone cliff along the coast with a central plateau about 60 metres above sea level, and a coastal terrace about 500 metres wide. A coral reef surrounds the island, with its only major break on the central west coast, near the capital, Alofi. The wet season runs from November to April and the dry season from May to October.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeological evidence suggests that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesians">Polynesians</a> from Samoa settled Niue around 900 CE. According to Niuean tradition, further settlers came from Tonga in the 16th century as a war party. Until the beginning of the 18th century the island seems to have had no national government or single leader, and chiefs and heads of families held authority over parts of the population. A succession of patu-iki, or kings, then ruled, beginning with Puni-mata. Tui-toga, who reigned from 1875 to 1887, was the first of them to adopt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><p>The next documented European visitors represented the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-missionary-society">London Missionary Society</a>. After many years of trying to land a European missionary, they abducted a Niuean named Nukai Peniamina and trained him as a pastor at the Malua Theological College in Samoa. He returned in 1846 and, after attempts in other villages had failed, was allowed to land at Mutalau, whose chiefs protected him day and night at the fort in Fupiu.</p><p>In 1889 the chiefs of Niue wrote to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> asking her to stretch out your mighty hand, that Niue may hide herself in it and be safe, and expressed anxiety that another nation might take the island; the British did not at first take up the offer. A petition by Cook Islanders in 1900 asking for annexation included Niue if possible. The New Zealand Parliament restored self-government in 1974 with the Niue Constitution Act, after a referendum in which Niueans chose among independence, self-government and continuation as a New Zealand territory; the majority chose self-government. Robert Rex was elected by the Niue Assembly as the first Premier in 1974 and held the post until his death 18 years later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Niue is a self-governing island country in free association with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, and its people are citizens of New Zealand. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary democracy</a> divided into 14 villages, each sending a member to the Niue Assembly. Niue is bilingual, with Niuean and English spoken, and a large majority of Niueans live in New Zealand.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Niue” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Māori moved from rural communities to the cities on a large scale after the Second World War, and from the 1960s migrants from Samoa, the Cook Islands, Tonga and <b>Niue</b> made Auckland the largest Polynesian city in the world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>With the consent of leading ariki, New Zealand annexed the islands of the Cook Islands Federation, together with <b>Niue</b>, on 11 June 1901.</blockquote><a href="/state/cook-islands">Cook Islands</a></li><li><blockquote>With the agreement of Makea and other ariki, the islands, together with <b>Niue</b>, were annexed to New Zealand on 11 June 1901, becoming the Cook Islands.</blockquote><a href="/state/cook-islands-federation">Cook Islands Federation</a></li></ul></section>
